{"kind":"request","text":"A card's press outlasts the bridge's 10 seconds, and the card then refuses every press\n\nScope: J2's acceptance, 2082efd9. Hugh's third session, 13:39 EST on main 237d61a6. The card rendered in the desktop AI app for the first time. Clicking the title showed the buttons.\n\nHugh pressed View diff. The card said \"tools/call: the host did not reply. No action was retried.\" The next press said \"The displayed card changed. Read it again; no new act was sent.\"\n\nWhat the log and the journal show (`~/Library/Logs/Claude/mcp-server-dap-views.log`, destination `sc_cxzc5esn`):\n- `tools/call` id 9 arrived at 18:43:46.948Z and answered at 18:43:58.528Z, so it took 11.58 s.\n- `read-token` by the owner's key landed at destination entry 60 at 18:43:52.730Z, with its outcome at entry 61. So that press did sign, and the read did finish.\n- `tools/call` ids 10, 11 and 12 at 18:44:35 to 18:44:48 each answered in about 1 ms.\n- `packages/mcp/browser/bridge.ts:33` gives every host request 10 seconds.\n- From reading: the late answer moved the view's cut in the server, the card kept the old cut, and `session.ts:187` then refuses each press as stale. The app gives a person no way to read it again.\n\nDo this:\n1. A press's request must outlast a read. Set the wait from the bound of the slowest read this server can make, and say what bound you chose. Find why this press took 5.8 s to sign and 11.58 s in all, against the 1.6 to 3.5 s of the scripted runs. A first session under this home is one candidate.\n2. After a timeout, the card must recover by reading the view again. A stale refusal must do the same, or offer a button that does. A person must never be left where every press refuses.\n3. Correct the words. \"No action was retried\" hides that this press signed a `read-token`. Say what the card knows: the request may have gone, and a permanent read may have been signed.\n4. Show the timeout case in a test with a read that outlasts the wait, with a control, and cite the card workflow's run.\n5. Take the two usability findings from this session: the title shows no sign that it opens anything, and the card's summary shows a raw `sha256:` digest and `acts.review-verdict / acts.merge` as its only content. Hugh looked at that card and reported \"no buttons\".\n\nOrder: first. Move the person's steps to that head, and tell me.","body":{"conditions":"A card press outlasts a read and recovers from a timeout or a stale refusal, its words state what may have been signed, the title reads as openable, under a test, a control and a cited workflow run, approved by the checker and landed on main.","target_head":"237d61a6a5b71cdd2b933c93ae3b38eb74d5746f","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}